===Etymology 1===
{{etymon|en|id=vessel|:inh|enm:pot|tree=1}}
From {{inh|en|enm|pot}}, {{m|enm|potte}}, from {{inh|en|ang|pott||pot}} and {{der|en|fro|pot|t=pot}} (probably from {{der|en|frk|*pott}}); both Old English and Frankish from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*puttaz||pot, jar; tub}}, of unknown origin.

Cognate with {{cog|stq|Pot||pot}}, {{cog|nl|pot||pot}}, {{cog|nds-de|Pott||pot}}, {{cog|de|Pott||pot}}, {{cog|sv|potta||chamber pot}}, {{cog|is|pottur||tub, pot}} (from {{cog|non|pottr||pot, tub, basin}}), and perhaps {{cog|hy|պույտ||pot}} (from {{cog|xcl|պոյտն||pot, earthen pot}}) and {{cog|sq|bocë|t=bottle, decanter}}, related either via late {{der|en|ine-pro|-}}<!--*bewd- is problematic--> or as a ''{{lg|Wanderwort}}''. {{m+|enm|pot}} gave rise to {{cog|ga|pota}}, {{cog|gd|poit}}{{,}} and {{cog|cy|pot|id=jar}} through {{lg|borrowing}}. Further cognates in {{cog|roa}} languages are via {{cog|la-lat|pottus//pōtus}}, {{m|la|pottum}}, which either was borrowed from the Germanic word or, less likely, is its source; see more on the Proto-Germanic entry.

The sense of {{m-g|ruin}} or {{m-g|deterioration}} was originally a general allusion to “being chopped up and tossed in a (normally fiery) pot, like a piece of meat” (i.e. “to get wasted or done with (by someone)”). The {{m-g|clean}} slang term which was used in reference to toilet rooms and lavatories apparently derives from English [[chamberpot]]s, although now usually encountered as {{m|en|potty}} in the context of children's toilet training.

