# {{senseid|en|feathers}}{{lb|en|ornithology|uncountable}} The [[feather#Noun|feathers]] [[cover#Verb|covering]] the [[body#Noun|body]] of a [[bird#Noun|bird]] [[collectively]].
#: {{syn|en|pennage|q1=obsolete}}
#* {{RQ:Turberville Faulconrie|author=Vicentino|chapter=Out of the Italian Concerning the Goshawkes and Their Kinds|page=65|passage=Very fayre ⁊ hardy Hawkes {{quote-gloss|“Slauon Goshawkes”}}, large footed, very well penned, theyr downe and '''plumage''' excellent fine, their tungs blacke, and their nares large and wide.}}
#* {{RQ:Drayton Muses Elizium|page=112|passage=[T]he glad Bird {{quote-gloss|a dove}} ſtayes all the day abroad, / And vvondrous proud that he a place had found, / {{...}} / Bathing himſelfe, vvhich long he had not done, / And dryes his feathers in the vvelcome Sunne, / Pruning his '''plumage''', clenſing euery quill, / And going back, he beareth in his bill / An Oliue leafe, by vvhich ''Noah'' vnderſtood / The great decreaſe and vvaning of the flood: {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Willughby Ray Ornithology|chapter=Of the Eagle in General|section=I (Of Rapacious Diurnal Birds)|page=57|passage=[W]ith a ſvveat ſhe caſts her feathers; and is then carefully nurſed up and fed, till ſhe recover her '''plumage''' again.}}
#* {{RQ:Darwin Origin of Species|page=89|passage=The rock-thrush of Guiana, birds of Paradise, and some others, congregate; and successive males display their gorgeous '''plumage''' and perform strange antics before the females, which standing by as spectators, at last choose the most attractive partner.}}
#* {{RQ:Darwin Descent of Man|volume=II|page=187|passage=In some few cases the young {{quote-gloss|birds}} in their first '''plumage''' differ from each other according to sex; the young males resembling more or less closely the adult males, and the young females more or less closely the adult females.}}
#* {{RQ:Stevenson Treasure Island|chapter=In the Enemy's Camp|page=228|passage=The parrot sat, preening her '''plumage''', on [[w:Long John Silver|Long John {{quote-gloss|Silver}}]]'s shoulder.}}
#* {{RQ:Eddison Worm|page=33|passage=Somewhat like a heron she was, but stouter, and shorter of leg, and her beak shorter and thicker than the heron’s; and so long and delicate was her pale gray '''plumage''' that hard it was to say whether it were hair or feathers.}}
#* {{quote-av|en|year='''1969''' December 7 (first broadcast)|author=w:John Cleese; w:Graham Chapman|episode=Full Frontal Nudity [the {{w|Dead Parrot sketch}}]|title=[[w:Monty Python’s Flying Circus|Monty Python’s Flying Circus]]|season=1|number=8|passage=''Shopkeeper'' ({{w|Michael Palin}}): No, no, he's not dead, he's, he's restin'! Remarkable bird, the Norwegian Blue, idn'it, ay? Beautiful '''plumage'''! / ''[[w:Mr Praline|Mr. Praline]]'' (John Cleese): The '''plumage''' don't enter into it. It's stone dead.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}}
## {{lb|en|archaic|uncountable}} Feathers [[use#Verb|used]] [[ornamentally]]; [[feathering#Noun|feathering]]; {{lb|en|countable}} a [[bunch#Noun|bunch]] or [[tuft#Noun|tuft]] of such feathers; a [[plume#Noun|plume]].
##* {{RQ:Carlyle Friedrich|volume=II|chapter=Visit to Dresden|page=38|passage=[S]he could not but contrast the splendour of the Polish retinues and their '''plumages''' and draperies, with the strait-buttoned Prussian dignitaries, all in mere soldier uniform, succinct 'blue coat, white linen gaiters,' and no superfluity even in the epaulettes and red facings.}}
## {{lb|en|falconry|uncountable|obsolete}} Feathers which are [[feed#Verb|fed]] to a [[hawk#Noun|hawk]] to [[cause#Verb|cause]] it to [[regurgitate#Verb|regurgitate]], for the [[purpose#Noun|purpose]] of [[clean#Verb|cleaning]] its [[crop#Noun|crop]]; a [[type#Noun|type]] of [[casting#Noun|casting]].
##* {{RQ:Turberville Faulconrie|author=Vicentino|chapter=Certayne Speciall Poyntes, Necessarie for a Falconer, or Ostreger, Collected out of the Italian Author|page=76|passage=Euery night, after he hath fleen with his hawke the daye, either at the fielde, or brooke, he muſte giue his hawke caſting, ſomewhyles '''plumage''', ſome other whiles pellets of Cotton, or ſuche like, and agayne, ſometimes ſome one medicine or other, according as by hir caſting, or mewte, he ſhall perceyue hir to ſtande needefull thereof, {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|figurative|uncountable}}
## A [[person#Noun|person]]'s [[dress#Noun|dress]], especially when [[elaborate#Adjective|elaborate]] or [[fine#Adjective|fine]].
##* {{RQ:Stowe Uncle Tom's Cabin|volume=I|chapter=Tom's Mistress and Her Opinions|page=248|passage=While she was thinking what to say, Marie gradually wiped away her tears, and smoothed her '''plumage''' in a general sort of way, as a dove might be supposed to make toilet after a shower, {{...}}}}
## A person's [[external#Adjective|external]] [[appearance]], especially when [[showy]].
##* {{RQ:Wordsworth Prelude|page=147|passage=I have thought / Of thee, thy learning, gorgeous eloquence, / And all the strength and '''plumage''' of thy youth, {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Scott Peveril of the Peak|volume=I|chapter=XII|page=289|passage=This expression of liberality and confidence from a man of Major Bridgenorth's reserved and cautious disposition, gave full '''plumage''' to Mistress Deborah's hopes; {{...}}}}

